# Biography of 張釋之 and 馮唐

張釋之， who served as Commandant of Justice, was a native of 堵陽， with the courtesy name 季. He had an elder brother named 仲 with whom he lived. Through his wealth he became a cavalry gentleman-in-attendance, serving Emperor 孝文. For ten years he received no promotion and became known to no one. 釋之 said: "Serving long in office has diminished 仲's property, without success." He wished to resign and return home. The General of the Palace Attendants 袁盎， knowing his worthiness, regretted that he should leave, and so requested that 釋之 be transferred to fill the post of palace usher. After 釋之 had completed his audience, he thereupon presented before the emperor matters of expediency. Emperor 文帝 said: "Be humble; do not make lofty arguments—make them such that they can be implemented today." Thereupon 釋之 spoke for a long time about affairs between the Qin and Han dynasties—why Qin lost and why Han flourished. Emperor 文帝 praised him, and thereupon appointed 釋之 as Chief of the Palace Ushers.

釋之 accompanied the emperor on an outing, ascending the tiger enclosure. The emperor asked the Warden of the Shanglin Park about the registers of the various birds and beasts—more than ten questions—and the warden looked left and right, entirely unable to answer. The Overseer of the Tiger Enclosure from the side answered in the warden's place all the emperor's questions about the registers of birds and beasts in great detail, wishing thereby to display his ability to answer orally in response like an echo without end. Emperor 文帝 said: "Should an official not be like this? The warden is worthless!" Thereupon he decreed that 釋之 appoint the overseer as Governor of Shanglin Park. 釋之 after a long while stepped forward and said: "What sort of person does Your Majesty consider the Marquis of 絳， 周勃？" The emperor said: "An elder." He asked again: "What sort of person is the Marquis of 東陽， 張相如？" The emperor again said: "An elder." 釋之 said: "The Marquis of 絳 and the Marquis of 東陽 are called elders, yet these two men in discussing affairs could not even get the words out of their mouths—how could one emulate this overseer's chattering, glib tongue and quick replies! Moreover, Qin, by employing its knife-and-brush officials, saw its officials compete with one another in prizing swiftness and harsh scrutiny, yet their failing was merely that it was all empty formality, without the substance of compassion. For this reason, their faults went unheard, and decline continued until the Second Emperor, when the realm crumbled. Now if Your Majesty, on account of this overseer's eloquence, promotes him beyond his station, I fear the realm will follow the wind and drift along, competing in eloquence without substance. Moreover, those below are transformed by those above more swiftly than shadow and echo; promotion and dismissal cannot but be examined carefully." Emperor 文帝 said: "Good." Thereupon he stopped and did not appoint the overseer.

The emperor got into his carriage and summoned 釋之 to ride as attendant, and proceeding slowly, asked 釋之 about the failings of Qin. 釋之 reported everything in full detail. Upon reaching the palace, the emperor appointed 釋之 as Prefect of the Public Carriages.

Shortly after, the Crown Prince and the King of 梁 shared a carriage and entered for audience, not dismounting at the Sima Gate. Thereupon 釋之 pursued and stopped the Crown Prince and the King of 梁， forbidding them to enter the palace gate. He then impeached them for irreverence in not dismounting at the public gate, and memorialized this. When 薄太后 heard of it, Emperor 文帝 removed his cap and apologized, saying: "I failed to instruct my sons properly." 薄太后 then sent an envoy with a decree of pardon for the Crown Prince and the King of 梁， and only then were they allowed to enter. From this Emperor 文帝 considered 釋之 remarkable and appointed him Grand Master of the Palace.

Shortly after, he rose to General of the Palace Attendants. He accompanied the emperor to 霸陵， where they sat on the north side facing the precipice. At that time Lady 慎 was in attendance. The emperor pointed out to Lady 慎 the road to 新豐， saying: "This is the road that leads to 邯鄲." He had Lady 慎 play the se zither, while the emperor himself leaned on the zither and sang. His feelings were sorrowful and melancholy, and turning to his ministers he said: "Alas! If with the stones of the northern mountain an outer coffin were made, with ramie floss cut and layered, and lacquer applied between them, who could ever disturb it!" Those in attendance all said: "Excellent." 釋之 stepped forward and said: "If there is something desirable within it, even if sealed in the Southern Mountain there would still be cracks; if there is nothing desirable within it, even without a stone coffin, what sorrow would there be!" Emperor 文帝 praised him. Afterward he appointed 釋之 as Commandant of Justice.

Shortly after, the emperor was going out through the Middle Wei Bridge when a man came running out from beneath the bridge, startling the imperial horses. Thereupon the emperor sent riders to arrest him and handed him over to the Commandant of Justice. 釋之 interrogated him. The man said: "I came from the county, heard the imperial procession's clearing of the roads, and hid beneath the bridge. After a long while, I thought the procession had already passed, so I came out, saw the imperial carriages and horsemen, and ran." The Commandant of Justice submitted his judgment: one man violated the imperial procession's cleared road; the penalty should be a fine. Emperor 文帝 angrily said: "This man personally startled my horses. My horses fortunately were gentle—if they had been other horses, would they not surely have injured me? Yet the Commandant of Justice judges only a fine!" 釋之 said: "The law is that which the Son of Heaven shares in common with the realm. Now the law is thus, yet to make it heavier is to make the law untrustworthy to the people. Moreover, at that moment, had Your Majesty had him executed on the spot, that would have been the end of it. But now that the matter has been handed down to the Commandant of Justice—the Commandant of Justice is the balance of the realm; should it tilt once, then all the laws used in the realm would be made heavier or lighter accordingly—where would the people place their hands and feet? I only ask Your Majesty to examine this." After a long while, the emperor said: "The Commandant of Justice's judgment is correct."

Afterward, someone stole the jade rings placed before the seat in the Ancestral Temple of 高帝. He was caught, and Emperor 文帝 in anger handed him down to the Commandant of Justice for trial. 釋之, examining the law that one who steals objects used by the ancestral temple for clothing and service, submitted a memorial, judging that the man should be executed in the marketplace. The emperor was greatly angered and said: "That a man should be so without the Way as to steal the vessels of the former emperor's temple—I handed him to you, the Commandant of Justice, intending to have his clan exterminated, yet you memorialize judging him by the law. This is not my intention in reverently serving the ancestral temples." 釋之 removed his cap, knocked his head on the ground, and apologized, saying: "By law, this is punishment enough. And though the crimes are of the same degree, they are differentiated according to rebellion or obedience. Now if for stealing vessels of the ancestral temple one's clan is exterminated, then if—though it be one chance in ten thousand—some ignorant commoner should take a single handful of earth from 長陵， what could Your Majesty add to his punishment beyond this law?" After a long while, Emperor 文帝 spoke of it with the Empress Dowager, and then permitted the Commandant of Justice's judgment. At that time, the Commandant of the Capital, the Marquis of 條， 周亞夫， and the Chancellor of 梁， the Marquis of 山都， 王恬開， seeing that 釋之 held his judgments impartially, formed close friendships with him. From this, Commandant 張 was praised throughout the realm.

Later, when Emperor 文帝 died and Emperor 景帝 ascended the throne, 釋之 was afraid and pleaded illness. He wished to resign and leave, but feared great punishment would arrive; he wished to appear and apologize, but did not know how it would go. Following the plan of 王生， he finally appeared and apologized, and Emperor 景帝 did not blame him.

王生 was one who was skilled in the words of Huang-Lao, a scholar living in retirement. He was once summoned to stand in the court, where the Three Excellencies and Nine Ministers all gathered and stood. 王生, an old man, said: "My sock has come untied," and turning to Commandant 張 said: "Tie my sock for me!" 釋之 knelt and tied it. After this was done, someone said to 王生： "Why did you alone humiliate Commandant 張 in court, making him kneel and tie your sock?" 王生 said: "I am old and lowly, and I judge that in the end I can be of no benefit to Commandant 張. Commandant 張 is at present a famous minister of the realm, so I deliberately humiliated the Commandant, making him kneel and tie my sock, wishing thereby to enhance his reputation." When the various lords heard of this, they considered 王生 worthy and esteemed Commandant 張 all the more.

Commandant 張 served Emperor 景帝 for a year or more, and became Chancellor to the King of 淮南， still on account of his former fault. After a long while, 釋之 died. His son was 張摯， with the courtesy name 長公， whose office reached Grand Master, and then he was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate himself to the men of his age, he never took office again for the rest of his life.

馮唐's grandfather was a man of 趙. His father moved to 代. When Han arose, they moved to 安陵. 唐 was noted for filial piety and became Chief of the Palace Attendant Corps, serving Emperor 文帝. When Emperor 文帝 passed by in his carriage, he asked 唐： "Elder, how did you come to be a palace attendant? Where is your home?" 唐 answered everything truthfully. Emperor 文帝 said: "When I lived in 代， my Supervisor of the Imperial Kitchen 高袪 repeatedly told me of the worthiness of the 趙 general 李齊， who fought at the foot of 鉅鹿. Now whenever I eat, my thoughts are never far from 鉅鹿. Do you know of him?" 唐 replied: "He was still not the equal of 廉頗 and 李牧 as generals." The emperor said: "How so?" 唐 said: "My grandfather, when in 趙， was an officer-commander and was friendly with 李牧. My father formerly was Chancellor of 代 and was friendly with the 趙 general 李齊， and knew the sort of man he was." When the emperor had heard of the character of 廉頗 and 李牧， he was greatly pleased, and slapping his thigh said: "Alas! Why can I not have a general like 廉頗 or 李牧 in my time? Then why would I worry about the Xiongnu!" 唐 said: "I beg your pardon, but even if Your Majesty obtained 廉頗 and 李牧， you would not be able to use them." The emperor was angered, rose, and went into the inner palace. After a long while, he summoned 唐 and rebuked him: "How could you humiliate me before the crowd? Could you not have found a private place?" 唐 apologized: "I am a rustic who does not know what to avoid."

At that time, the Xiongnu had newly made a great incursion into 朝那， killing the Commandant of 北地， 卬. The emperor, concerned about the Hu raiders, thereupon again asked 唐： "How do you know that I cannot use 廉頗 and 李牧？" 唐 replied: "I have heard that when the kings of antiquity dispatched generals, they knelt and pushed the wheel of the chariot, saying: 'Within the gates, I, the sovereign, control matters; beyond the gates, the general controls them. Military merits, ranks, and rewards are all decided in the field, and reported upon return.' This is no empty saying. My grandfather told me that when 李牧 was a general of 趙 stationed on the frontier, the rents of the army market were all used by him himself to feast his soldiers; rewards and gifts were decided in the field, without interference from the center. Entrusted with authority and held responsible for success, 李牧 was thus able to exert fully his wisdom and ability: he dispatched thirteen hundred selected chariots, thirteen thousand mounted archers, and a hundred thousand men worth a hundred pieces of gold each. Thus he pursued the Chanyu in the north, defeated the 東胡， destroyed the 澹林， checked mighty Qin in the west, and held off 韓 and 魏 in the south. At that time, 趙 nearly became hegemon. Later it happened that the 趙 king 遷 came to the throne; his mother had been a singing girl. When King 遷 was enthroned, he heeded the slander of 郭開， and in the end executed 李牧， ordering 顏聚 to replace him. Therefore the army was broken and the soldiers routed, and 趙 was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have privately heard that 魏尚， Governor of 雲中， uses all the rents of his army market to feast his soldiers and officers, and from his private stipend money, every five days slaughters an ox to feast his guests, army officers, and retainers. Therefore the Xiongnu keep far away, not approaching the passes of 雲中. Once when raiders did enter, 尚 led chariots and horsemen to strike them, and the number he killed was great. Now the soldiers are all sons of ordinary families, rising from the fields to join the army—how would they know the registers and tallies? All day they fight with all their strength, cutting off heads and capturing prisoners; when their merits are reported to the staff headquarters, if a single word does not correspond, the civil officials punish them according to law. Their rewards are not given, while the officials' enforcement of the law is certain to be applied. In my foolishness, I consider that Your Majesty's laws are too strict, rewards too light, punishments too heavy. Moreover, Governor 魏尚 of 雲中 was charged with an error of six ranks in reporting heads and prisoners taken; Your Majesty handed him to the officials, stripped his rank, and sentenced him to penal labor. From this it follows that even if Your Majesty obtained 廉頗 and 李牧， you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish; I have touched upon what should be avoided; I deserve death, I deserve death!" Emperor 文帝 was pleased. That very day he ordered 馮唐 to bear the staff of authority and pardon 魏尚， restoring him as Governor of 雲中， and appointed 唐 as Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the chariots and horsemen of the Commandant of the Capital and the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor 景帝 ascended the throne, and made 唐 Chancellor of 楚， then dismissed him. When Emperor 武帝 ascended the throne, he sought worthy and good men, and 馮唐 was recommended. 唐 was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so 唐's son 馮遂 was made a palace attendant. 遂's courtesy name was 王孫； he too was a remarkable man, and was on friendly terms with me.

The Grand Historian says: 張季 spoke of elders and upheld the law without bending to the emperor's wishes; 馮公's discourse on generals and commanders—how full of flavor! How full of flavor! The saying goes: "If you do not know a man, look at his friends." What these two gentlemen praised and celebrated could be inscribed in the halls of state. The Book of Documents says: "Without partiality, without partisanship, the royal way is broad and level; without partisanship, without partiality, the royal way is smooth and even." 張季 and 馮公 approached this ideal. [end of text]


